ublk-shared-cancel.c reuses that implementation for two loop devices sharing
the same backing file, as in stress_04. Both children must acknowledge actual
queued I/O before the supervisor releases their running-daemon kill paths.
It covers pairs of explicit-zero-copy, automatic-zero-copy and per-I/O modes.
Fio logs are device-specific; the barrier itself is bounded and setup failures
cannot become kernel-cancellation PASSes.

Initial shared run:
  GCC: 0 PASS / 1 FAIL, later 2 cases NOT RUN. Both devices report queued I/O,
       all six client/writeback children finish with expected EIO, but one
       kublk del hits the diagnostic's eight-second bound (wait status 31744).
       Shutdown completes, no blocked writeback is captured before shutdown.
  EvoCC: 3/3 pairs PASS, normal shutdown.
This is a deletion-latency RED, not yet the original 45-second/writeback stall.
Do not identify it as an EvoCC miscompile or claim a root cause from the retry.
